  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1140 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe forecast period begins with the upper ridge over the western \nDakotas and Nebraska Panhandle and northwest flow over the Great \nPlains. A weak shortwave is moving around the ridge and will\narrive over the area later tonight/early Wednesday morning. The\nbest chance (20-30%) for showers/storms will be across \nsoutheastern Nebraska and southwestern Iowa. At this time, \nsevere weather is not expected. However, several models are \nshowing precipitable water values of 1.75\"+ over southeastern \nNebraska through tomorrow morning. Any storms that are able to \ndevelop may produce heavy rain which could lead to some \nlocalized flash flooding. \n\nWednesday, the ridge shifts slightly east, over the central Dakotas \nas a trough comes ashore over the PACNW. This will be a system to \nwatch as it works its way across the CONUS, as it is expected to be \nour next storm system. A few lingering showers/storms may be \npossible in southeastern Nebraska early Wednesday morning, clearing \nout by mid-morning. Temperatures will climb into the upper 80s to \nlow 90s in the afternoon. Areas, particularly north of Hwy 92 may \nsee some hazy skies through the day as wildfire smoke filters into \nthe region from the northwest. \n\nWednesday night into Thursday, a series of disturbances move across \nthe region bringing chances for showers and storms. Any morning \nconvection may impact the severe weather potential expected later in \nthe day. The main wave is expected to move into the region Thursday \nevening/night with warm, gulf moisture moving into the region. A few \nstorms may become strong to severe with gusty winds, hail, and \nlocally heavy rainfall being the main concerns. Precipitable water \nvalues continue to look pretty high for eastern Nebraska and western \nIowa. Flash flooding may be a concern as well Thursday \nevening/night. \n\nThe upper wave will slide east, over central and western Iowa \nand Minnesota Friday. Expect additional showers and \nthunderstorms Friday morning, gradually ending west to east by \nearly afternoon. Enjoy some cooler temperatures with Friday \nhighs topping out in the low to mid-80s and Saturday morning \nlows in the low to mid-60s.\n\nThe cooler weather sticks around through the weekend with a drier \npattern arriving. Saturday, an upper trough arrives over British \nColumbia and the Aleutian Islands. Meanwhile, an upper ridge nudges \neast over the Dakotas, with northerly flow setting up over the Great \nPlains. Through the weekend, a few models are showing low pressure \ndeveloping and pushing into Saskatchewan and Manitoba, with the \nridge being pushed over Minnesota and high pressure setting up over \nthe Four Corners. Expect a pleasant weekend with highs in the low to \nmid-80s and lows in the 60s. \n\nDry weather and gradual warming is expected heading into the start \nof next week.",
